====================================================================================================================
mutation controls
====================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: a_0 ten times too large moves the whole ladder by the factor sqrt(10) the deep-MOND kernel demands, so eta really is measuring the acceleration scale and not an arbitrary normalisation   (median eta with 10 a_0 = 0.73 vs 2.15, ratio 0.340 against the predicted 0.316)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: with nu = 1 (Newton, no kernel) the same systems need a mass discrepancy of 42 in groups and 9 in rich clusters instead of eta ~ 2, so the kernel removes 80-95% of the missing mass everywhere on the ladder and the residual is what is left   (Newtonian M500/M_bar median 12.4 (groups 42.1, rich clusters 9.4) vs eta = 2.15 with the kernel)
  [PASS] M3 mutation: shuffling the gas masses between systems destroys the ladder -- the mass trend of eta swings to the value a random baryon budget gives, which is far from the measured one   (shuffled d log eta/d log M500 = +0.709 against the real -0.082)

====================================================================================================================
the LambdaCDM alternative computed beside it
====================================================================================================================
  in LambdaCDM the quantity with a prediction is the baryon fraction, not eta: f_bar should approach the cosmic 0.157
                    mass bin  f_bar(measured)   f_bar/f_cosmic   missing baryons
     GROUPS      [12.5,13.5)            0.024            0.151             84.9%
     poor groups [13.5,14.0)            0.053            0.338             66.2%
     clusters    [14.0,14.5)            0.081            0.516             48.4%
     rich        [14.5,15.0)            0.106            0.673             32.7%
     very rich   [15.0,15.6)            0.132            0.841             15.9%
  so LambdaCDM's own statement about groups -- that they should have LOST most of their baryons to feedback -- and
  the framework's -- that the residual should switch on with the hot gas -- point in OPPOSITE directions on the same
  numbers: the systems with the fewest baryons are the ones the framework needs to work best in.
  [PASS] 55-6 the two theories are pulled the same way by the same number and neither is discriminated here: groups are missing 4/5 of their cosmic baryons in LambdaCDM's accounting, and it is exactly that missing gas that keeps the framework's eta above 1 in groups.  The measurement cannot separate 'feedback expelled the gas' from 'the gravity law is wrong'   (f_bar = 0.024 in groups vs 0.106 in rich clusters, against a cosmic 0.157)

====================================================================================================================
  VERDICT on item 55.
    * The lensing group Delta-Sigma profiles the item names are not machine-readable anywhere reachable from here,
      so the group rung is built on eRASS1's weak-lensing-CALIBRATED masses rather than on a stacked profile.  That
      is a real weakening and it is recorded as one.
    * There is NO STEP.  eta at R500 is flat from 5e12 to 1.6e15 solar masses: d log eta/d log M500 lies between
      -0.08 and +0.14 across both footings and every defensible baryon budget, a factor of at most 1.6 over 2.5
      decades, against the +0.30 dex/dex a 1 -> 2 step across one decade would need.
    * And the sign of the answer is against the framework: groups do not have eta = 1, they have eta = 1.4 to 2.9.
      The residual is present at group masses, not switched on at cluster masses.
    * eta(group) CANNOT be pinned to +-0.2, which was the item's Kepler-grade bar: the three defensible stellar
      budgets give 1.56 to 2.89 at 1e13 because at group masses the unmeasured stars are comparable to the measured
      gas.  Stellar masses for eRASS1 groups are the measurement that would decide it.
    * A by-product: the same fit independently reproduces item 68's redshift liability, +0.18 to +0.21 dex per unit
      z at fixed mass, where a constant a_0 requires zero.

